  <name>Hush</name>
  <summary>Type with your voice</summary>
  <developer id="io.github.andymai">
    <name>Andy Aragon</name>
  </developer>

  <description>
    <p>
      Hush turns your speech into text on the Linux desktop. Hold a key, talk,
      and let go, and your words appear wherever you're typing. Hush runs
      OpenAI's Whisper models or NVIDIA's Parakeet on your own computer, on the
      GPU when you have one, so your audio stays with you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Flatpak works on KDE Plasma 6 and GNOME 48 or later. Your desktop
      sets the hotkeys and asks once for keyboard access. On Sway, Hyprland,
      and other wlroots desktops, install a package from the project page
      instead.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Hold to talk, or tap twice for hands-free recording that transcribes in parts while you speak</li>
      <li>Any language that Whisper knows, with an optional key for each language</li>
      <li>Text that fits the application: casual in chat, full sentences in mail, no line breaks in a terminal</li>
      <li>Your own words: teach Hush a name once, and it spells the name your way</li>
      <li>An optional tidy-up by a local or cloud language model, which sees only the text, never your audio</li>
      <li>A history of your transcripts that only you can read, and recovery of a failed recording</li>
      <li>A settings window and a panel icon</li>
    </ul>
  </description>

  <launchable type="desktop-id">io.github.andymai.hush.desktop</launchable>
  <provides>
    <binary>hush</binary>
  </provides>

  <url type="homepage">https://github.com/andymai/hush</url>
  <url type="bugtracker">https://github.com/andymai/hush/issues</url>
